Arthur Evans accepted it as authentic, many other scholars rejected it as a forgery, and more recent scholars have again argued for its authenticity. The article says it examines the find circumstances, finds them suspicious, introduces semantic coherence as a criterion, and argues that the ring's 
coherence depends on Egyptian prototypes and that the butterfly motif weighs against authenticity."}, {"id": "20260721171428448284_48b5eed3", "type": "Statement", "confidence": "medium", "qualifiers": ["The document frames these observations as supporting authenticity."], "source_id": 
"20260721171428309240_2f1491e3", "text": "New technical observations presented in the document support the ring's authenticity and counter previous arguments against it.", "statement_key": "statement:28d21c974a3b3bffc49718ee", "document_id": "20260721171428364295_66a36c51", "limitations": ["The 
