"The evidence establishes that the Ring of Nestor is a Late Minoan / early Late Bronze Age gold signet ring (CMS VI no. 277), described in scholarly and catalog-style captions as a Minoan/Kakovatos gold signet ring showing four cult scenes. Scholarly work (Evans 1925; later studies including 
Eliopoulos and Vlachopoulos) links it to a royal treasure of gold rings and bead-seals from a Mycenaean rock tomb near Thisbe in Boeotia, found in 1915 by a peasant in a chamber tomb excavated in the rock by the village of Dombrena, and notes that only the discovery context is securely documented 
while later ownership or museum-repository chains remain unspecified. Other scholarship dates the ring to the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age (16th–15th century BC), with a caption giving a Late Minoan range of circa 1700–1450 BC; this dating is said to rest on comparative iconography and on 
detailed study of techniques and craftsmanship. The iconography is characterized as an elaborate multi-scene cult program, with its key pictorial unit (Evans’s ‘watch-dog’ or ‘dragon’ guarding a ‘tree of the world’) reinterpreted by Vlachopoulos as a scorpion, described as a major apotropaic symbol in
Bronze Age Aegean art that ‘kills’ the ring’s so‑called Minoan dragon, showing that the ring’s imagery and motifs have been closely analyzed. Multiple scholarly sources document substantial disagreement about authenticity and interpretation: early and mid‑20th‑century authors condemned the ring as a 
forgery (including arguments from Egyptian or butterfly motifs and suspicious find circumstances), while more recent technical, stylistic, and comparative studies argue for its authenticity and semantic coherence with securely ancient Aegean material. A secondary-hosted caption (via Heritage 
