[tools:eeb7ce20-ef8a-ccd0-a4d2-d1b0d78e921d] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 4, 'title': 'Dating, provenance, and ownership', 'content': 'In Evans’s original account, the Ring of Nestor formed part of a treasure of gold rings and bead seals discovered in 1915 in a Mycenaean 
rock-cut chamber tomb near the site of Thisbe in Boeotia, close to the village of Dombrena.[11] The discovery was reported as having been made by a local peasant, and Evans emphasized both the richness of the gold assemblage and the presence of associated weapons and tools—bronze spearheads, a short 
sword, a bronze razor and a small perforated double-axe head of iron—found in the same group of tombs, though not all were authenticated as coming from the specific chamber containing the treasure.[11] Glass-paste ornaments with perforations for attaching pendant gold disks were also noted, and Evans 
related the type of these ornaments to material from Dimini, assigning them to an early Late Minoan III B date in Minoan terms.[11] This combination of objects provided the main basis for his chronological framing and for the attribution of the treasure, including the Ring of Nestor, to a Late Bronze 
Age Mycenaean context.\n\nSubsequent scholarship, working with a broader corpus of Aegean seal rings and with more recent excavated material, has refined the chronological range associated with the Ring of Nestor. Abstracts and image captions in the available sources describe the ring as belonging to 
"the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age", explicitly framing this as the sixteenth to fifteenth centuries BCE, and one image-caption source gives a more specific range of circa 1700–1450 BCE under the label "Late Minoan I".[6][8] These chronological assignments draw on comparative study of 
iconographic subjects across wall paintings, gold rings, figurines and ivories, as well as on more thorough examination of techniques and craftsmanship in Creto-Mycenaean signet rings, though the detailed technical and stylistic arguments are not included in the excerpts available here.[6] A scholarly
article on the ring’s authenticity also aligns its stylistic and manufacturing details with known Minoan–Mycenaean artifacts, presenting this as reinforcement for an ancient origin.[4]\n\nThe ring’s reported provenance and discovery circumstances have been central to debates about authenticity as well
as to reconstructions of its ownership history. Evans presented the treasure from Thisbe as a "truly royal" find from a Mycenaean rock tomb, but later authors have described the circumstances surrounding the discovery and subsequent handling of the so-called Nestor Ring as "obscure" or even 
"legendary", noting that this contributed to a tendency among some twentieth-century scholars to treat the ring, along with the Ring of Minos, as modern forgeries.[5][6][11] A study focused on the ring’s Egyptian iconography revisits the background of the find circumstances and judges them suspicious,
while adding a criterion of semantic coherence that emphasizes the dependence of the ring’s iconographic program on Egyptian prototypes and argues that specific motifs, such as the butterfly, weigh against authenticity.[5] In contrast, more recent work argues that this forgery-oriented tendency has 
been completely reassessed in favor of authenticity, citing new iconographic parallels and technical studies of Late Bronze Age signet rings as evidence that the Ring of Nestor can be situated securely within the sixteenth–fifteenth-century BCE Aegean glyptic tradition.[6][13]\n\nBeyond the initial 
find near Thisbe, the supplied material does not provide a continuous chain of documentation for the ring’s subsequent ownership and institutional history. Popular image-platform captions state that the Ring of Nestor is held by the Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, and describe it as a "Minoan 
Kakovatos signet ring" weighing 31.8 units, but no primary museum catalogue entry, inventory number or acquisition record is included in the evidence.[8] A Journal of Hellenic Studies article and the 1925 monograph document the discovery and early scholarly treatment of the ring, while later studies 
and exhibition narratives focus on contextualizing the object within the broader story of Mycenaean Pylos and Aegean seal rings rather than tracing its full collection history.[6][10][11][12][14] As a result, current reconstructions of the ring’s dating and provenance rely on a combination of early 
reports from Boeotia, stylistic and technical comparisons with other Late Bronze Age artifacts, and secondary references to museum holdings whose details remain only partially documented in the material available here.[4][6][8][11][13]', 'section_type': 'body', 'evidence_ids': 
