[tools:eeb7ce20-ef8a-ccd0-a4d2-d1b0d78e921d] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 2, 'title': 'Discovery and archaeological context', 'section_type': 'body', 'content': 'In the published account that first brought the Ring of Nestor to wider attention, Arthur Evans presented it as 
part of a "royal treasure" of gold rings and bead seals found in 1915 in a Mycenaean rock tomb near the site of Thisbe in Boeotia, by the village of Dombrena.[11] Evans noted that the discovery was made by a peasant in a chamber tomb cut into the rock and that other metal finds such as bronze 
spearheads, a short sword, a bronze razor and a small perforated iron double-axe head were associated with the same group of tombs, although he distinguished between items seen or secured by himself and those not authenticated as belonging to the specific chamber that contained the treasure.[11] 
Glass-paste objects with perforations for the attachment of pendant gold disks also formed part of the assemblage, and Evans associated these with a chronological horizon corresponding to the Dimini jewels, which he described as early Late Minoan III B.[11] The ring itself subsequently became the 
subject of a separate monograph, "The Ring of Nestor" (London, 1925), in which Evans framed the object as offering "a glimpse into the Minoan after-world" and linked it to broader questions about Minoan and Mycenaean funerary art.[12][14]\n\nLater excavation and museum narratives situate the Ring of 
Nestor within a wider landscape of Mycenaean and Minoan interaction and of elite burial practice, though the provided material does not trace a continuous chain of custody from the 1915 discovery to a specific institutional holding.[11] The exhibition "The Pylos of Nestor. A Mycenaean Kingdom 
Revealed" at the National Archaeological Museum in Athens presents, as part of its story of Mycenaean Messenia, how tholos tombs and other grave types across sites such as Myrsinochori, Tragana, Koukounara, Koryphasio, Psari, Nichoria, Daras, Vlachopoulo, Chora (Volimidia), Peristeria and Antheia 
formed a complex funerary landscape reflecting social hierarchy and conceptions of the afterlife.[10] At Ano Englianos, near the Palace of Nestor, multiple tholos tombs, chamber tombs and two shaft graves, one being the grave of the so-called Griffin Warrior, have been excavated and integrated into 
this picture of local rulers and their burial traditions.[10]\n\nThe Griffin Warrior tomb near the Palace of Nestor at Pylos, discovered during renewed excavations by a University of Cincinnati team, contained hundreds of artifacts of gold, silver, bronze, ivory and semiprecious stones associated with
a single male buried in a stone-built Late Helladic IIA grave.[15][16] Among the gold artifacts were four signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual scenes, which have been discussed as indicators of Mycenaean engagement with Minoan iconography and as part of the elite display of the Griffin 
Warrior.[15][16] Although these rings are not identified in the provided evidence as including the Ring of Nestor itself, scholarship that revisits the Ring of Nestor’s authenticity and iconography makes explicit use of such finds—wall paintings, gold rings, figurines, ivories and seal stones—as 
comparative material for assessing the stylistic and technical plausibility of the Nestor ring within the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age.[6][10][15][16]\n\nIn this broader context, the Ring of Nestor functions as a bridge between early twentieth-century antiquarian discoveries in Boeotia and 
later, more systematically excavated Mycenaean contexts in Messenia. Its reported origin in a rock-cut tomb near Thisbe, combined with its elaborate iconography and its reassessment in light of recent finds from Pylos and other sites, has made it a touchstone in discussions about how Minoan glyptic 
imagery circulated in Mycenaean elite burials and how archaeologists handle objects whose discovery circumstances are only partially documented or considered "legendary" in retrospect.[6][10][11]', 'evidence_ids': ['20260721171444373000_1662904d', '20260721171444386862_f7316fdb', 
