[tools:eeb7ce20-ef8a-ccd0-a4d2-d1b0d78e921d] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 3, 'title': 'Description and iconography', 'section_type': 'body', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is consistently described in the supplied sources as a gold signet ring or gold intaglio, with one 
caption specifying that it belongs to the Late Minoan I period and giving a date range of circa 1700–1450 BCE.[6][8] Image-platform captions further state that it is a "Minoan Kakovatos signet ring" and attribute it to the Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, while reporting a weight of 31.8 units 
without specifying the unit of measurement.[8] Scholarly summaries identify the ring with CMS VI no. 277 in the Corpus der Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel, and one abstract notes that it is among the largest known Aegean signet rings, with AI-generated key takeaways listing its dimensions as 3.29 cm
by 2.18 cm and ranking it as the fifth largest in that corpus, although the underlying dataset for this ranking is not reproduced in the available material.[6] These descriptions situate the ring as a substantial, heavy gold object with a large oval bezel carrying a finely engraved 
scene.[6][8]\n\nPopular captions state that the bezel "shows four cult scenes", a characterization that accords with more general references to its iconography as consisting of multiple ritual episodes.[8] Evans’s terminology, echoed in later discussion, identifies a central guardian figure on the 
bezel—a "watch-dog" or "dragon"—that protects an old "tree of the world", whose branches host what has been called one of the most densely populated scenes in Aegean glyptic art.[6] The overall composition is thus understood to combine a central arboreal motif with attendant supernatural or protective
beings and human or humanoid figures engaged in ritual actions around altars or other cult equipment, although the provided material does not include a full scene inventory or photograph that would allow detailed enumeration of each element.[6][8]\n\nSubsequent scholarship has proposed more specific 
identifications of key motifs within this complex scene. One interpretation views the principal motif not as a dragon in its own right but as a scorpion, treated as a major apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography; in this reading, the scorpion "kills" the Minoan dragon of the ring and 
thereby provides a narrative of protective action around the tree and its associated cult context.[6] Other work emphasizes the importance of motifs such as butterflies and incurved altars in evaluating the ring’s imagery and its relationship to both excavated cult installations and possible external 
prototypes.[5][13] An article on the ring’s Egyptian iconography introduces the criterion of semantic coherence and argues that aspects of the ring’s iconographic program depend on Egyptian prototypes, suggesting that the presence and use of a butterfly motif count against the artifact’s authenticity 
by pointing to a modern reassembly of motifs rather than a Bronze Age Aegean composition.[5]\n\nThe iconography of the Ring of Nestor has therefore become a testing ground for broader questions in Aegean glyptic studies: how to distinguish authentic Bronze Age signet rings from modern forgeries that 
imitate Minoan style, how to weigh technical analysis against iconographic plausibility, and how to integrate comparative material such as wall paintings, other gold rings, figurines, ivories and seal stones in building or questioning iconographic narratives.[4][6][13] Technical and stylistic studies 
cited in the available abstracts highlight unique manufacturing details on the ring that are said to align it with known Minoan–Mycenaean artifacts, while at the same time debates over specific motifs—such as scorpions, butterflies, guardians and world trees—show how different readings of the same 
engraved scene can lead to contrasting assessments of the ring’s origin and meaning.[4][5][6][13]', 'evidence_ids': ['20260721171432158801_d99fcb0b', '20260721171432178616_677874ba', '20260721171933124698_a07adbb4', '20260721171449876731_f59bcbb6', '20260721171449890762_fa0ebaca', 
