[tools:eeb7ce20-ef8a-ccd0-a4d2-d1b0d78e921d] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 1, 'title': 'Overview', 'section_type': 'lead', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is a gold signet ring generally attributed to the Late Bronze Age Aegean world and more specifically to the Late Minoan I 
period, with dates in the available literature ranging around the sixteenth to fifteenth centuries BCE and, in one image-caption source, circa 1700–1450 BCE.[6][8] It is described in popular and image-library sources as a Minoan or Kakovatos signet ring showing four cult or ritual scenes, and is said 
there to be housed in the Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, although the provided material does not include a primary museum catalogue entry that would confirm inventory number or full acquisition history.[6][8] Scholarly discussions align the ring with the corpus of Creto-Mycenaean gold seal 
rings catalogued in the Corpus der Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel (CMS) series, where it corresponds to CMS VI no. 277 and is noted as one of the larger known Aegean signet rings.[6]\n\nSince Arthur Evans’s study on a treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals reportedly found in a Mycenaean rock
tomb near Thisbe in Boeotia, the Ring of Nestor has played a prominent role in debates over authenticity, forgery, and iconographic interpretation in Aegean archaeology.[12][14][11] Evans himself treated the ring as a genuine glimpse into a Minoan "after-world", while later scholars in the twentieth 
century at times regarded it as a modern or pseudo-Minoan creation, partly because of the obscure and even "legendary" circumstances under which the treasure was said to have been discovered.[5][6][11] More recent work has reassessed this tendency, presenting technical observations, comparative 
iconographic analysis and broader studies of Late Bronze Age signet-ring craftsmanship as supporting an ancient origin, though the strong phrasing in some abstracts and summaries may not capture the full range of current opinion.[4][6][13]\n\nIconographically, the ring’s bezel carries a complex scene 
that Evans described as including a guardian figure—a "watch-dog" or "dragon"—protecting a "tree of the world" whose branches host a densely populated array of figures, and later authors have referred to the overall program as consisting of multiple cult or ritual scenes.[6][8] Subsequent 
interpretations have suggested that the key motif should be identified as a scorpion, understood as an apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean art, and have explored possible relationships to Egyptian prototypes; the butterfly motif and the connection of incurved altars in excavated cult installations 
to the ring’s imagery have also entered discussions of how its scene should be read and whether that reading supports or undermines its authenticity.[5][6][13] The Ring of Nestor is therefore notable not only as a large and elaborately engraved gold signet ring, but also as a focal case in the study 
of Aegean glyptic art, where questions of dating, provenance, authenticity and iconographic meaning remain the subject of sustained scholarly attention.[4][5][6][11][13]', 'evidence_ids': ['20260721175521115048_e5a8b7c2', '20260721175245320391_5cdee027', '20260721175245233560_61ec494c', 
