[tools:eeb7ce20-ef8a-ccd0-a4d2-d1b0d78e921d] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 1, 'title': 'Overview', 'section_type': 'lead', 'content': 'The Ring of Nestor is a gold signet ring generally attributed to the Late Bronze Age Aegean world and more specifically to the first 
centuries of the Late Bronze Age, framed in one abstract as the sixteenth to fifteenth centuries BCE and in one image-caption source as belonging to the Late Minoan I period, circa 1700–1450 BCE.[6][8] Popular descriptions characterize it as a Minoan or Kakovatos signet ring showing four cult or 
ritual scenes and state that it is held by the Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, though the provided material does not include a primary museum catalogue entry that would confirm inventory number or full acquisition history.[8] Scholarly summaries identify the ring with CMS VI no. 277 in the 
Corpus der Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel and note, in an abstract and an associated platform summary, that it is one of the larger known Aegean signet rings, with reported dimensions of about 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm, even if the detailed basis for that size ranking is not reproduced in the available 
evidence.[6]\n\nThe ring entered scholarly discourse through Arthur Evans’s account of a treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals reportedly found in a Mycenaean rock-cut chamber tomb near the site of Thisbe in Boeotia, by the village of Dombrena, a discovery he dated to 1915 and described as a 
"royal" find from a peasant-excavated tomb.[11] Evans later devoted a monograph to "The Ring of Nestor", presenting it as a glimpse into a Minoan "after-world" and linking it to questions of Minoan and Mycenaean funerary and religious imagery.[12] In the decades that followed, however, some scholars 
regarded the ring, together with the Ring of Minos, as a modern or pseudo-Minoan creation, pointing to the obscure or "legendary" character of its find circumstances and, in some cases, associating it with suspected forgers or with the reuse of Egyptian iconographic prototypes.[5][6]\n\nMore recent 
work has reassessed this forgery-oriented tendency. A study of the ring’s authenticity emphasizes that new technical observations and unique stylistic and manufacturing details align the ring with known Minoan–Mycenaean artifacts, while a later chapter on "The Ring of Nestor and the quest for 
authenticity" argues that previous views of the Nestor and Minos rings as fakes, based on their unclear provenance, have been "completely" reassessed in favor of authenticity, supported by newly discovered iconographic parallels—such as wall paintings, gold rings, figurines and ivories—and by detailed
examination of Late Bronze Age signet-ring techniques and craftsmanship.[4][6] At the same time, a study on the "pseudo-Minoan Nestor Ring" and its Egyptian iconography judges the find circumstances suspicious, introduces a criterion of semantic coherence that emphasizes the ring’s dependence on 
Egyptian prototypes, and contends that the use of a butterfly motif argues against authenticity.[5] Platform-generated summaries of an article on the ring’s iconography further underline that contradictions in technical analyses have led some authors to rely heavily on iconographic arguments, 
including connections between incurved altars on the ring and excavated cult installations, and they note that aspects of the iconography have been read as reflecting cultural practices from the 1920s.[13]\n\nIconographically, the Ring of Nestor’s bezel carries a complex scene that Evans described as 
including a guardian figure—a "watch-dog" or "dragon"—protecting an old "tree of the world" whose branches host what has been called one of the most populous scenes in Aegean glyptic art, while popular captions refer more broadly to four cult scenes.[6][8] Later interpretations have proposed that the 
key motif should be identified instead as a scorpion, understood as a major apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography that "kills" the Minoan dragon of the ring, and have explored how this identification, together with other motifs such as butterflies and incurved altars, fits into 
comparative frameworks involving Egyptian and Aegean prototypes.[6][5][13] As a result, the Ring of Nestor is notable not only as a large and elaborately engraved gold signet ring, but also as a focal case in the study of Aegean glyptic art, in which questions of dating, provenance, authenticity and 
iconographic meaning remain actively discussed on the basis of both technical and iconographic evidence.[4][5][6][11][13][8]', 'evidence_ids': ['20260721175245267570_bac8607d', '20260721171445295579_97796f63', '20260721171931013657_15506814', '20260721175245442459_dc8a5768', 
