assemblage as a truly "royal" treasure. He listed associated finds from the same group of tombs, including bronze spearheads, a short sword, a bronze razor and a small perforated double-axe head of iron, while noting that not all of these were authenticated as coming from the specific chamber that 
contained the treasure.[11] Evans also mentioned a set of perforated glass-paste ornaments with holes for attaching pendant gold disks and related their type to the Dimini jewels, assigning them to early Late Minoan III B in his chronological scheme.[11] These observations provided the basis for his 
contextual and chronological framing of the treasure and the ring.\n\nEvans subsequently devoted a monograph to "The Ring of Nestor", describing it as offering a glimpse into a Minoan "after-world" and integrating it into broader discussion of Minoan and Mycenaean funerary art.[12][14] While the 
excerpts available here do not reproduce the full excavation and collection history beyond the initial discovery near Thisbe, they situate the ring within a pattern of Mycenaean burials enriched with elaborate seal rings and other prestige goods. A later Journal of Hellenic Studies extract reiterates 
