and two shaft graves, one being the grave of the Griffin Warrior, as part of the story of local rulers at Ano Englianos and their burial practices.[10] The exhibition narrative emphasizes that, from the seventeenth to the fourteenth centuries BCE, Messenia emerged as a major center of the Mycenaean 
world, with petty kingdoms and great wealth expressed through rich grave goods across sites such as Myrsinochori, Tragana, Koukounara, Koryphasio, Psari, Nichoria, Daras, Vlachopoulo, Chora (Volimidia), Peristeria and Antheia.[10]\n\nThe Griffin Warrior grave near the Palace of Nestor, discovered in 
