2.18 cm and describing it as the fifth largest known Aegean signet ring; the underlying dataset for this size ranking and the measurement methods are not detailed in the available material.[6]\n\nPopular image captions state that the Ring of Nestor "shows four cult scenes", and more general scholarly 
references similarly treat its iconography as a sequence of ritual episodes.[8] Evans’s terminology, echoed in later discussion, identifies a central guardian figure on the bezel—a "watch-dog" or "dragon"—that protects an old "tree of the world", whose branches host what he described as the most 
populous scene in Aegean glyptic art.[6] In this understanding, the ring’s composition combines a central arboreal motif with attendant protective or supernatural beings and multiple figures engaged in actions around altars or other cult equipment, producing an unusually dense and complex ritual 
tableau on a single signet ring.[6][8]\n\nLater interpretations have proposed more specific identifications and symbolic readings for key motifs within this scene. One abstract, summarizing an article on the ring’s iconography and authenticity, reports that the key pictorial unit of the Ring of 
