'20260721171428767758_96d7bd92', '20260721171428786469_5d3662de', '20260721171428802857_9aae40a6', '20260721171428821586_ce9404b6', '20260721171428846426_58e3a588', '20260721175245336832_3ea12032', '20260721171445371390_41c34e0b', '20260721175245492636_47b939eb', '20260721175245509695_d29da4ea', 
'20260721175245287595_76b69aa4', '20260721175245304299_aab73f60', '20260721175245353418_7043abe2', '20260721171445310694_70df3127', '20260721171915897174_61808cd1'], 'disclosed_conflict_ids': ['20260721175245542206_6ebe93d1']})
[tools:eeb7ce20-ef8a-ccd0-a4d2-d1b0d78e921d] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 2, 'title': 'Discovery and archaeological context', 'section_type': 'body', 'content': 'In the account that first brought the Ring of Nestor to wider attention, Arthur Evans presented it as part of a 
treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals discovered in 1915 in a Mycenaean rock-cut chamber tomb near the site of Thisbe in Boeotia, by the village of Dombrena.[11] Evans described the discovery as having been made by a peasant excavating chamber tombs in the rock and characterized the gold 
assemblage as a truly "royal" treasure. He listed associated finds from the same group of tombs, including bronze spearheads, a short sword, a bronze razor and a small perforated double-axe head of iron, while noting that not all of these were authenticated as belonging to the specific chamber that 
contained the treasure.[11] Evans also mentioned perforated glass-paste ornaments with holes for attaching pendant gold disks and related their type to the Dimini jewels, assigning them to early Late Minoan III B in his chronological framework.[11]\n\nEvans subsequently devoted a monograph to "The 
Ring of Nestor", describing the ring as offering a glimpse into a Minoan "after-world" and integrating it into broader discussion of Minoan and Mycenaean funerary art.[12][14] A later extract reiterates the 1915 date, the location near Thisbe and the Dombrena find spot, but explicitly notes that the 
source material documents only the discovery context for the treasure and does not provide a later ownership or museum-repository chain.[11] Within the evidence available here, the early history of the Ring of Nestor is therefore limited to this reported find in a Boeotian rock-cut 
tomb.\n\nArchaeological work in Messenia, particularly around the Palace of Nestor at Ano Englianos, provides a broader funerary and palatial landscape against which the Ring of Nestor has been contextually compared. The National Archaeological Museum’s exhibition "The Pylos of Nestor. A Mycenaean 
Kingdom Revealed" presents multiple tholos tombs, chamber tombs and two shaft graves, one being the grave of the so-called Griffin Warrior, as part of the story of local rulers and their burial practices in Messenia between the seventeenth and fourteenth centuries BCE.[10] The exhibition narrative 
emphasizes that wealthy principalities in areas such as Myrsinochori, Tragana, Koukounara, Koryphasio, Psari, Nichoria, Daras, Vlachopoulo, Chora (Volimidia), Peristeria and Antheia developed complex funerary landscapes in which rich grave goods expressed social hierarchy and conceptions of the 
afterlife.[10]\n\nThe Griffin Warrior grave near the Palace of Nestor, discovered by a University of Cincinnati team in renewed excavations, contained hundreds of artifacts of gold, silver, bronze, ivory and semiprecious stones associated with a single male buried in a stone-built Late Helladic IIA 
tomb.[15][16] Among the gold artifacts were four signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual scenes; abstracts and figure captions show their positions in the grave plan and present drawings of one ring’s impressions and bezel, illustrating how Minoan-style iconography was used on Mycenaean seal rings 
in this securely excavated context.[15][16] Although the sources presented here do not identify any of these Griffin Warrior rings as the Ring of Nestor itself, a later abstract on the Ring of Nestor’s authenticity notes that recent finds of comparable iconographic subjects—such as wall paintings, 
gold rings, figurines and ivories—have significantly reinforced the reassessment of the ring’s authenticity and place it among unique signet rings of the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age.[6]\n\nTaken together, Evans’s report of a 1915 find near Thisbe and later excavation-based studies from 
Pylos and Messenia place the Ring of Nestor within a broader archaeological picture of elite burials and palatial culture in the Mycenaean world. The ring’s reported origin in a rock-cut tomb in Boeotia, its subsequent treatment as a key object in discussions of Minoan afterlife imagery, and its 
comparison with securely excavated Late Helladic graves such as the Griffin Warrior tomb have made it a touchstone in debates on how Minoan glyptic imagery circulated in Mycenaean contexts and how archaeologists handle objects whose early discovery circumstances are only partially 
documented.[6][10][11][15][16]', 'evidence_ids': ['20260721171444373000_1662904d', '20260721171444386862_f7316fdb', '20260721171444400558_995bdafc', '20260721171444414607_5e343cac', '20260721171935853975_1c9193a1', '20260721171935891830_14922521', '20260721171445248409_3be2e427', 
