[tools:eeb7ce20-ef8a-ccd0-a4d2-d1b0d78e921d] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 3, 'title': 'Description and iconography', 'section_type': 'body', 'content': 'In the supplied sources, the Ring of Nestor is consistently described as a gold signet ring or gold intaglio. An abstract 
discussing Aegean signet rings places its craftsmanship within "the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age (16th–15th century BC)", while an image-caption source labels it as a Late Minoan I ring dated circa 1700–1450 BCE.[6][8] The same caption calls it a "Minoan Kakovatos signet ring" and reports a 
weight of 31.8 units, although the unit is not specified.[8] Scholarly summaries identify the ring with CMS VI no. 277 in the Corpus der Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel and, in platform-generated key takeaways, note that it is one of the largest Aegean signet rings, with dimensions reported as 3.29 
cm by 2.18 cm; the underlying dataset and measurement methods are not detailed in the available material.[6]\n\nPopular image captions state that the Ring of Nestor "shows four cult scenes", and more general scholarly references similarly treat its iconography as a sequence of ritual episodes.[8] 
Evans’s terminology, echoed in later discussion, identifies a central guardian figure on the bezel—a "watch-dog" or "dragon"—that protects an old "tree of the world", whose branches host what he described as the most populous scene in Aegean glyptic art.[6] In this understanding, the ring’s 
composition combines a central arboreal motif with attendant protective or supernatural beings and multiple figures engaged in actions around altars or other cult equipment, producing an unusually dense and complex ritual tableau on a single signet ring.[6][8]\n\nLater interpretations have proposed 
more specific identifications and symbolic readings for key motifs within this scene. One abstract, summarizing an article on the ring’s iconography and authenticity, reports that the key pictorial unit of the Ring of Nestor, which Evans called a "watch-dog" or "dragon", is reidentified as a scorpion,
described as a major apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography that "kills" the Minoan dragon of the ring.[6] In this reading, the scorpion motif emphasizes the protective function of the scene and aligns it with other apotropaic imagery in Aegean art.[6] Platform-generated key points 
reiterate that the scorpion plays a crucial role in the ring’s iconography, although the detailed comparative material used to support this identification is not reproduced in the excerpts available here.[6]\n\nOther scholars focus on motifs such as butterflies and incurved altars when assessing the 
ring’s iconography and its implications for authenticity. A synopsis of an article on the Nestor ring’s Egyptian iconography states that the study introduces semantic coherence as a criterion in the authenticity debate, arguing that coherence in the ring’s imagery depends on Egyptian prototypes and 
that the use of a butterfly motif counts against authenticity.[5] Platform-generated summaries attached to an article on the ring’s iconography highlight contradictions in technical analyses and report that connections between incurved altars depicted on the ring and excavated Aegean cult 
installations have been interpreted as supporting its authenticity; they also note that, in some readings, the ring’s iconography may reflect cultural practices from the 1920s.[13]\n\nWithin this material, the Ring of Nestor’s iconographic program functions as a test case for broader questions in 
Aegean glyptic studies. Technical and stylistic studies cited in abstracts present the ring’s manufacturing details as aligning with known Minoan–Mycenaean artifacts, while iconographic analyses debate whether motifs such as scorpions, butterflies, guardians and world trees belong to a coherent Bronze
Age Aegean repertoire or to a modern assemblage drawing on Egyptian and Minoan models.[4][5][6][13] The description of the ring as a large gold signet showing four cult scenes, the emphasis on a central guardian or scorpion motif, and the dense population of figures around a tree and altars together 
make its bezel one of the most discussed single scenes in Aegean glyptic art in the sources available here.[6][8]', 'evidence_ids': ['20260721175245267570_bac8607d', '20260721171445295579_97796f63', '20260721171931013657_15506814', '20260721171445218060_338f71fb', '20260721171449876731_f59bcbb6', 
