[tools:eeb7ce20-ef8a-ccd0-a4d2-d1b0d78e921d] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 4, 'title': 'Dating, provenance, and ownership', 'section_type': 'body', 'content': 'In Evans’s original account, the Ring of Nestor formed part of a treasure of gold signet rings and bead seals 
discovered in 1915 in a Mycenaean rock-cut chamber tomb near the site of Thisbe in Boeotia, close to the village of Dombrena.[11] Evans reported that the discovery was made by a peasant excavating chamber tombs in the rock and emphasized both the richness of the gold assemblage and the presence of 
associated weapons and tools—bronze spearheads, a short sword, a bronze razor and a small perforated double-axe head of iron—found in the same group of tombs, although he distinguished between items he had seen or secured and those not authenticated as belonging to the specific chamber that contained 
the treasure.[11] He also described perforated glass-paste ornaments with holes for attaching pendant gold disks and related their type to the Dimini jewels, assigning these ornaments to early Late Minoan III B in his chronological framework.[11]\n\nLater scholarship, drawing on a wider corpus of 
Aegean glyptic material, has refined the chronological range associated with the Ring of Nestor. An abstract in a volume on Aegean iconography refers to "unique signet rings of the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age (16th–15th century BC)" and presents recent iconographic parallels and technical 
studies as reinforcing the reassessment of the ring’s authenticity within this timeframe.[6] A non-scholarly image caption labels the Ring of Nestor as belonging to the Late Minoan I period and gives a date range of circa 1700–1450 BCE, while describing it as a "Minoan Kakovatos signet ring"; the 
caption does not explain the dating method or specify analytical criteria.[8] A descriptive text attached to a study of the ring’s authenticity states that new technical observations support the ring’s authenticity and that its stylistic and manufacturing details align with known Minoan–Mycenaean 
artifacts, although the detailed technical data are not reproduced in the material available here.[4]\n\nThe ring’s reported provenance and discovery circumstances have been central to debates about authenticity as well as to reconstructions of its ownership history. Evans’s account presents the 
treasure from Thisbe as a truly "royal" find from a Mycenaean rock tomb, but later authors describe the circumstances in which the Ring of Nestor and the Ring of Minos were found as "obscure" or even "legendary", and a chapter on authenticity links earlier tendencies to treat these rings as modern 
forgeries to this uncertainty.[6][11] An article on the ring’s Egyptian iconography specifically examines the background of the find circumstances and judges them suspicious, while introducing semantic coherence as a criterion in the authenticity debate and arguing that coherence in the ring’s 
iconography depends on Egyptian prototypes in ways that, together with the use of a butterfly motif, argue against authenticity.[5] In contrast, the later chapter on the Ring of Nestor and the quest for authenticity contends that this forgery-oriented tendency has been reassessed in favour of 
authenticity, citing recent iconographic parallels and more thorough study of signet-ring techniques and craftsmanship in the sixteenth–fifteenth centuries BCE as evidence.[6]\n\nBeyond the initial discovery near Thisbe, the excerpts provided here do not supply a continuous chain of documentation for 
the ring’s subsequent ownership and institutional history. An extract from the Journal of Hellenic Studies article notes that the available source material documents only the discovery context for the treasure and does not give a later ownership or museum-repository chain.[11] Popular image-platform 
captions state that the Ring of Nestor is held by the Ashmolean Museum, University of Oxford, and describe it as a "Minoan Kakovatos signet ring" weighing 31.8 units, but no primary museum catalogue entry, inventory number or acquisition record appears in the evidence.[8] As a result, reconstructions 
of the ring’s dating and provenance within this set of sources rely on a combination of Evans’s early report from Boeotia, stylistic and technical comparisons with other Late Bronze Age artifacts, and secondary references to museum holdings whose details remain only partially 
documented.[4][6][8][11]', 'evidence_ids': ['20260721171444373000_1662904d', '20260721171444386862_f7316fdb', '20260721171444400558_995bdafc', '20260721171444414607_5e343cac', '20260721171935853975_1c9193a1', '20260721171935891830_14922521', '20260721171445248409_3be2e427', 
