[tools:eeb7ce20-ef8a-ccd0-a4d2-d1b0d78e921d] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 2, 'title': 'Discovery and archaeological context', 'section_type': 'body', 'content': 'In the sources available here, the Ring of Nestor enters modern discussion through its inclusion in the Corpus 
der Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel as CMS VI no. 277 and through later studies that treat it as part of a wider treasure of Aegean gold signet rings.[6][11] An extract from the Journal of Hellenic Studies describes a "royal" treasure of gold rings and bead‑seals found in a Mycenaean rock‑cut tomb 
near the site of Thisbe in Boeotia, by the village of Dombrena, and indicates that this find was made in 1915 by a peasant excavating chamber tombs in the rock.[11] The same extract notes associated finds such as bronze spearheads, a short sword, a bronze razor and a perforated double‑axe head of 
iron, and mentions perforated glass‑paste ornaments for attaching pendant gold disks, which are compared to Dimini jewellery and assigned to an early Late Minoan III B date in Minoan terms.[11] Within the excerpt, this discovery context is presented as the background for the group of rings to which 
the Ring of Nestor belongs, while explicitly stating that the available material documents only the discovery and not a later ownership or museum‑repository chain.[11]\n\nSubsequent work has shifted attention from the Boeotian tomb to broader Mycenaean contexts in Messenia, especially around the 
Palace of Nestor at Ano Englianos, as comparative settings for understanding the Ring of Nestor and related signet rings. An exhibition narrative on "The Pylos of Nestor" describes how, from the seventeenth to the fourteenth centuries BCE, Messenia saw the development of principalities whose rich 
tholos tombs, chamber tombs and shaft graves—such as the grave of the Griffin Warrior—formed a complex funerary landscape expressing social hierarchy and conceptions of the afterlife.[10] In this narrative, multiple sites across Messenia are presented as yielding wealthy burials with seal rings and 
other prestige goods, providing a securely excavated comparison set for older finds like the Ring of Nestor.\n\nThe Griffin Warrior grave near the Palace of Nestor, discovered and published in detail by a University of Cincinnati team, contained hundreds of artifacts of gold, silver, bronze, ivory and
semiprecious stones associated with a single male buried in a stone‑built Late Helladic IIA tomb.[15][16] Among the gold artifacts were four signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual scenes, whose positions in the grave and detailed drawings of impressions and bezels are illustrated in figures and 
described in abstracts.[15][16] A later abstract on the Ring of Nestor’s authenticity notes that recent finds of comparable iconographic subjects—including wall paintings, gold rings, figurines and ivories—have significantly reinforced the reassessment of the Ring of Nestor’s authenticity and place it
among unique signet rings of the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age.[6] In this way, excavated material from Pylos and Messenia serves as comparative context for the Ring of Nestor’s iconography and technical characteristics.\n\nWithin this limited evidentiary framework, the Ring of Nestor’s 
discovery history is therefore documented at the level of an early twentieth‑century report of a treasure from a Mycenaean tomb near Thisbe, while its archaeological context in current discussions relies heavily on comparison with better‑documented Mycenaean burials such as the Griffin Warrior grave 
and the wider funerary landscape of Messenia.[6][10][11][15][16]', 'evidence_ids': ['20260721171444373000_1662904d', '20260721171444386862_f7316fdb', '20260721171444400558_995bdafc', '20260721171444414607_5e343cac', '20260721171935853975_1c9193a1', '20260721171935891830_14922521', 
