[tools:eeb7ce20-ef8a-ccd0-a4d2-d1b0d78e921d] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 2, 'title': 'Discovery and archaeological context', 'section_type': 'body', 'content': 'In the sources available here, the Ring of Nestor enters modern discussion through its inclusion in the Corpus 
der Minoischen und Mykenischen Siegel as CMS VI no. 277 and through studies that treat it as part of a wider treasure of Aegean gold signet rings.[6][11] An extract from a Journal of Hellenic Studies article describes a "royal" treasure of gold rings and bead‑seals found in a Mycenaean rock‑cut tomb 
near the site of Thisbe in Boeotia, by the village of Dombrena, and notes that this discovery was made by a peasant excavating chamber tombs in the rock.[11] The same extract lists associated finds such as bronze spearheads, a short sword, a bronze razor and a perforated double‑axe head of iron, and 
mentions perforated glass‑paste ornaments for attaching pendant gold disks which are compared to Dimini jewellery and assigned to an early Late Minoan III B date in Minoan terms.[11] Within that excerpt this discovery context is presented as the background for the group of rings to which the Ring of 
Nestor belongs, while explicitly stating that the available material documents only the discovery and not a later ownership or museum‑repository chain.[11]\n\nSubsequent work shifts attention from the Boeotian tomb to broader Mycenaean contexts in Messenia, especially around the Palace of Nestor at 
Ano Englianos, as comparative settings for understanding the Ring of Nestor and related signet rings. An exhibition narrative on "The Pylos of Nestor" describes how multiple tholos tombs, chamber tombs and shaft graves, including the grave of the Griffin Warrior, formed a complex funerary landscape in
Messenia between the seventeenth and fourteenth centuries BCE, with rich grave goods expressing social hierarchy and conceptions of the afterlife.[10]\n\nThe Griffin Warrior grave near the Palace of Nestor, discovered by a University of Cincinnati team in renewed excavations, contained hundreds of 
artifacts of gold, silver, bronze, ivory and semiprecious stones associated with a single male buried in a stone‑built Late Helladic IIA tomb.[15][16] Among the gold artifacts were four signet rings decorated with Minoan ritual scenes; abstracts and figure captions show their positions in the grave 
plan and present drawings of one ring’s impressions and bezel, illustrating how Minoan‑style iconography was used on Mycenaean seal rings in this securely excavated context.[15][16] A later abstract on the Ring of Nestor’s authenticity notes that recent finds of comparable iconographic subjects—such 
as wall paintings, gold rings, figurines and ivories—have significantly reinforced the reassessment of the Ring of Nestor’s authenticity and place it among unique signet rings of the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age.[6]\n\nWithin this limited evidentiary framework, the Ring of Nestor’s discovery
history is therefore documented at the level of an early twentieth‑century report of a treasure from a Mycenaean tomb near Thisbe, while its archaeological context in current discussions relies heavily on comparison with better‑documented Mycenaean burials such as the Griffin Warrior grave and the 
wider funerary landscape of Messenia.[6][10][11][15][16]', 'evidence_ids': ['20260721171444373000_1662904d', '20260721171444386862_f7316fdb', '20260721171444400558_995bdafc', '20260721171444414607_5e343cac', '20260721171935853975_1c9193a1', '20260721171935891830_14922521', 
