[tools:eeb7ce20-ef8a-ccd0-a4d2-d1b0d78e921d] [AIMessage → tool] commit_report_chapter({'chapter_id': 3, 'title': 'Description and iconography', 'section_type': 'body', 'content': 'In the supplied sources, the Ring of Nestor is consistently described as a gold signet ring or gold intaglio. An abstract 
discussing Aegean signet rings places its craftsmanship within "the first centuries of the Late Bronze Age (16th–15th century BC)", while an image caption labels it as a Late Minoan I ring dated circa 1700–1450 BCE and calls it a "Minoan Kakovatos signet ring" showing four cult scenes.[6][8] The same 
caption reports a weight of 31.8 units, although the unit is not specified, and it does not give analytical details.[8] Platform‑generated notes identify the ring with CMS VI no. 277 and describe it as one of the larger Aegean signet rings, with dimensions reported as 3.29 cm by 2.18 cm.[6]\n\nPopular
image captions state that the Ring of Nestor "shows four cult scenes", and more general scholarly references similarly treat its iconography as a sequence of ritual episodes.[8] An abstract on the ring’s iconography and authenticity reports that the key pictorial unit of the Ring of Nestor, which 
Evans described as a "watch‑dog" or "dragon" guarding an old "tree of the world" whose branches host a populous scene, is reinterpreted as a scorpion, described as a major apotropaic symbol in Bronze Age Aegean iconography that "kills" the Minoan dragon of the ring.[6] Platform‑generated key points 
reiterate that the scorpion plays a crucial role in the ring’s iconography, even though detailed comparative material supporting this identification is not reproduced in the excerpts available.[6]\n\nOther iconographic discussions focus on motifs such as butterflies and incurved altars. A synopsis of 
an article on the Nestor ring’s Egyptian iconography states that the study introduces semantic coherence as a criterion in the authenticity debate, arguing that coherence in the ring’s imagery depends on Egyptian prototypes and that the use of a butterfly motif counts against authenticity.[5] 
Platform‑generated summaries attached to Theodore Eliopoulos’s article "Some Observations on the Iconography of the ‘Ring of Nestor’" highlight contradictions in technical analyses and report that connections between incurved altars depicted on the ring and excavated Aegean cult installations have 
been interpreted as supporting its authenticity.[13]\n\nWithin this limited evidentiary framework, the Ring of Nestor’s iconographic program functions as a test case for broader questions in Aegean glyptic studies. Technical and stylistic studies cited in abstracts present the ring’s manufacturing 
details as aligning with known Minoan–Mycenaean artifacts, while iconographic analyses debate whether motifs such as scorpions, butterflies, guardians and world trees belong to a coherent Bronze Age Aegean repertoire or to a modern assemblage drawing on Egyptian and Minoan models.[4][5][6][13] The 
description of the ring as a large gold signet showing multiple cult scenes, together with the emphasis on a dense guardian‑and‑tree composition, makes its bezel one of the more intensively discussed single scenes in the Aegean glyptic corpus represented in these sources.[6][8]', 'evidence_ids': 
